{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Take the millstones.</b> For thus is the custom of female slaves, and the meaning is that she shall go into captivity." "<b>Flour.</b> It is not possible; rather it is called by the name of its end result, and like it: ובגדי ערומים <small>(Job 22:10)</small> — strip. n: 'and like it' introduces a parallel case of a word whose semantic range extends to its outcome; the gloss 'strip' renders Ibn Ezra's implied reading of the Job phrase, which he cites as analogous." "<b>Uncover your locks.</b> It is the hair that is at the front of the head." "<b>שובל.</b> It is the hair that descends upon the cheeks, and it is called thus after the manner of משבולת הנהר <small>(Isaiah 27:12)</small>, according to the opinion of R. Moses ha-Kohen, but the correct view is that there is no difficulty with it, and its interpretation is according to its context without proof." "<b>Pass over the rivers.</b> To grind."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:58:24.675180096Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}